From 306d14d036be8cb088a3215bf98639be3593cecf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:10:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Validate item 1-1's capture layout, and close both gaps it
 exposed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 1-1's remaining half is blocked on one stated thing: a captured name's
Box[] index is decided by LambdaRewriter from the tree, and a deferred body has
none, so the layout must come from source alone. 0101 built the walk that would
derive it. This builds the checker that decides whether the derivation is
correct, and answers it.

The property is asymmetric: over-approximating costs a box per creation site and
that name's numeric tier, while under-approximating means a deferred body
resolves a name to a box that is not there — a miscompile. One missed capture
disqualifies the approach, so the checker exists to make a zero mean something.
Eleven fixtures, one of which deliberately records an empty prediction against a
non-empty truth and asserts the miss is reported.

Two structural obstacles the item's statement does not name.

ClosureRepository holds two populations, and "which variables this lambda
captures" means one of them: bindings handed in from an enclosing scope (Setup,
index >= 0) and the lambda's own locals that something nested captures (Convert,
index == -1). A free-name walk answers the first and correctly does not name the
second, so compared against the whole repository the simplest possible fixture
reports `outer` missing `q` — a function missing its own local. The deferral
needs two derivations, not one. And every function is handed a ScriptInfo_*
binding no source identifier names, so the layout needs a reserved region for
compiler-introduced captures.

Then the instrument had to be made to agree with itself. Repeats are recognised
rather than counted — Mandreel relays 1 336 of its 1 358 sites twice, with 0
disagreements, so the repeat is duplication rather than the rewrite deciding
differently. Undeferrable bodies are excluded: a Dynamic body has no set to be
right about, and recording it as an empty prediction reported every one of its
captures as a miss.

That left two real defects, both about a function's own name.

A function DECLARATION's name is bound in the ENCLOSING scope, so a
self-reference is free and a deferred body must be handed a box for it.
EnterFunction bound it inside the function as well, under a comment describing
only the named-function-expression case: 138 sites across five corpora reported
as capturing nothing.

And a named function EXPRESSION's name, which the specification does bind
inside, is materialised by this engine as a JSVariable parameter in the
enclosing scope that the body captures — so the layout must carry it even though
it is correctly not free. The binding is registered with a deliberately null
Variable and exposed through EvalCaptureExpression alone, which is what a first
attempt testing `Variable != null` missed. Item 0097's rule, ask what the
compiler built rather than what the analysis proved, deciding a mechanism rather
than a measurement for the first time.

Adding the self-name unconditionally over-approximated 126 sites, so FreeNameScan
now gives it a scope of its own — where the specification puts it too — and
reports SelfNameReferenced exactly. A parameter of the same spelling shadows it
correctly, and a reference reaching it can be told from one reaching a parameter.

Result on the corpus: zero missed sites of 5 157 checked, and 2 445 exact, which
is the same precision as before the self-name was predicted at all. The
derivation over-approximates on 2 712 sites — safe, and the cost side the
mechanism must be measured against.

The deferral mechanism itself remains unbuilt and remains L. Checker off by
default (BROILER_JS_CAPTURE_LAYOUT_CHECK=1); the FreeNameScan changes are
unconditional, because they are correctness. Compiler suite green: 1 252 tests,
including all 23 of 0101's own fixtures and its independent oracle.

